hello and thank you again for joining us  for this NCBI minute today we will  explore the safe search feature which  can provide automatic email updates on a  daily weekly or monthly basis and  conclude with creating a customized name  collection from our search results that  can be made public to share with others  let's get started with saving and  managing a search the easiest way to  begin is at the PubMed homepage located  at w WM AQ V if you're not already  signed into NCBI don't worry you'll be  prompted later for my example I am  automating my PubMed search for at  lesson COPD step one is to enter my  query or search terms if you're  interested in this tutorial on how to  construct a query information will be  provided at the end of this presentation  but in general the pubmed process is  from left to right and dates and ranges  are not recommended for save searches  step 2 click the search button next  create alert which is located beneath  the search bar a pop-up window will  appear again if you are not logged into  your NCBI account you will be prompted  to sign in now it's time to save the  search name the default is the search a  couple of things to remember the save  search name may be up to 100 characters  long and this will become the search  line of your email queries can become  quite cumbersome so I updated it next is  the original search query from PubMed  you are also able to modify your search  terms from here if you edit your search  terms always remember to click test  search terms this will provide the  number of items based on the edited  search although I do not modify my  search it shows that there are 3131  results found at the time of the  screenshot do you want your new search  results sent automatically to your email  I do so yes please select  we will discuss a little later what  happens if you say no thanks  next visually confirm your email address  remembering that automatic email updates  can only be sent to email addresses that  have been confirmed if an email bounces  back three times the email address will  be changed to unconfirmed also you may  consider adding nih.gov  to your email safe list if you do not  know how to do this please contact your  email providers customer support for  assistance to verify that your email is  confirmed click on your username or  change as you can see the email address  has been confirmed otherwise a link  would appear allowing you to resend the  confirmation email okay  now let's return to save searches and  schedule the frequency of the email for  monthly choices include the first Sunday  through Saturday the first day of the  month the first week day of the month  weekly and finally daily next we will  select the format the choices are  summary abstract and MEDLINE let's take  a quick look at an actual email the  examples shown are from Outlook 2016 all  emails consist of the name of the save  search and in this one I re-edit it the  name senders message is optional text  that can be included in each email the  date sent the PubMed search terms the  ability to view the complete results in  PubMed the ability to edit the search  settings and of course unsubscribed  and finally the PubMed email results in  summary format here is an example of the  same result and how it appears in the  abstract format and in the MEDLINE  format back to our customized Save  Search  let the maximum number of items to  appear in the email in order to verify  that the email alert is running properly  send even when there aren't any new  search results in the event an update is  not sent out as scheduled or incomplete  the next scheduled update will include  the missing items here is where you can  include any optional text that you wish  to be added to your email and click Save  you will be immediately taken back to  the PubMed search page and will see the  confirmation here you will also be able  to edit the search at any time as needed  earlier I pointed out that you could  click no thanks to the email search  results let's take a quick look at my  NCBI where the saved search is stored  since we are logged into NCBI click the  my NCBI link located next to your  username as you can see in the safe  search portlet there are two PubMed  searches the one we just created a  lesson COPD and another lung cancer for  lung cancer I selected no thanks I do  not want email updates  the what's new column shows that there  are 220 citations currently available  since I last looked at my results three  days ago  what's new is available for all searches  for example if adolescent COPD is  delivered on a weekly basis I can still  log into NCBI to see if there were any  added citation since my last email run  and view the citations by clicking on  the numbered link the same search  portlet also has the ability to manage  saved searches from here you can quickly  look at your scheduled email delivery  and edit by clicking either the gear or  scheduled link if you check the box next  to your search name you are able to  delete the selected item or items and  also see what's new now let's talk about  building a collection by going back to  the my NCBI home page  my NCBI comes with the default  collections of my bibliography other  citations and favorites today we will be  creating a customizable titled  collection to share with others for your  convenience we have great tutorials on  our default collections that will be  included at the end of this presentation  although we are starting from save  searches you may also conduct a search  directly in PubMed remember if you click  on lung cancer beneath the PubMed search  column it will rerun the search and  display the results in PubMed  likewise if I click the link under  what's new I will have access to only  the 220 new citations based on my search  from here we are taken to PubMed which  consists of my new save search results  select a few of the citations for the  collection and click send to if all 